      ],
      "review": {
        "verdict": "rework",
        "summary": "Alignment state precedence contradicts the required behavior.",
        "findings": [
          {
            "severity": "major",
            "file": "internal/cli/cmd_status.go",
            "line": 600,
            "title": "Unconfirmed alignment incorrectly renders verdicts",
            "detail": "VerdictsPresent is checked before !Confirmed. The task requires skipped → unconfirmed → confirmed without verdicts → verdict counts. An alignment containing verdicts while Confirmed is false must render “N clauses awaiting confirmation,” but this code renders the verdict summary. The tests omit this combination."
          }
        ],
        "provider": "copilot",
        "model": "gpt-5.6-sol"
      },
      "internal": [
        {
          "severity": "major",
          "file": "internal/cli/cmd_status.go",
          "line": 596,
          "title": "alignmentLine reuse breaks loadCommitMessage's \"skipped leaves message unchanged\" contract",
          "detail": "alignmentLine(a) now returns a non-empty string (\"skipped\", \"N clauses awaiting confirmation\", or \"N clauses confirmed, verdicts pending\") for states that previously produced \"\". internal/cli/cmd_next.go:539-552 loadCommitMessage explicitly reuses this same alignmentLine (per its own doc comment, unmodified in this diff) and relies on it returning \"\" whenever there are no verdict artifacts: 'No verdict artifacts (the audit is disabled, skipped, or has not run yet) leaves the message unchanged.' That contract is now broken for the reachable 'explicitly skipped' path: takt answer --gate agent_invalid --choice skip → skipAlignment(bdir, st) sets alignment.json's Skipped=true; decidePlan's ClausesPresent/ClausesConfirmed/VerdictsPresent facts all become true via the `|| a.Skipped` OR (internal/cli/facts.go:78-80), so the plan→execute transition proceeds immediately and loadCommitMessage is called with a.Skipped=true. alignmentLine now hits `case a.Skipped: return gateSkipped` and returns \"skipped\" (non-empty), so the commit subject/body now gets \" — alignment: skipped\" appended, directly contradicting the still-present, unmodified doc comment and the existing behaviour TestLoadCommitMessageOmitsAlignmentWhenAbsent pins for the 'no verdict artifacts' cases (that test only covers the fully-absent alignment.json case, not the explicit-skip case, so it does not catch this). The two other new non-empty branches ('N clauses awaiting confirmation' / 'N clauses confirmed, verdicts pending') cannot actually be reached from decidePlan's transition gate, so only the Skipped branch is a live regression, but it is a real, untested one.",
          "lenses": [
            "correctness"
          ]
        },
        {
          "severity": "blocking",
          "file": "internal/cli/cmd_status.go",
          "line": 598,
          "title": "alignmentLine's new non-empty branches break loadCommitMessage's documented 'no verdicts → unchanged' contract",
          "detail": "Task 4 rewrote alignmentLine (used by both status and, per its own doc comment at cmd_status.go:593, by cmd_next.go's loadCommitMessage) so the `Skipped` case now returns the literal \"skipped\" instead of \"\". loadCommitMessage (internal/cli/cmd_next.go:524-553) treats an empty alignmentLine result as \"no verdict artifacts (the audit is disabled, skipped, or has not run yet) — leave the message unchanged\" (its own doc comment says so verbatim) and otherwise appends \" — alignment: \u003cline\u003e\" to the plan→execute commit subject/body. skipAlignment (internal/cli/cmd_answer.go:307-314) writes alignment.json with Skipped=true and no verdicts, and decide.go's decidePlan (internal/decide/decide.go:331-341) treats Skipped as satisfying ClausesPresent/ClausesConfirmed/VerdictsPresent, so the run proceeds straight to ActLoadPlan without ever gaining verdicts. The next `takt next` therefore reaches loadCommitMessage with a non-nil, Skipped alignment digest, and the plan→execute commit now reads \"...— alignment: skipped\" — contradicting loadCommitMessage's own documented behaviour and permanently altering commit history for a path task 4 never mentions touching. No test exercises this: TestLoadCommitMessageOmitsAlignmentWhenAbsent (internal/cli/cmd_next_test.go:346) only covers alignment fully disabled (statusAlignment returns nil), not the skipped-with-clauses case.",
          "lenses": [
            "consistency"
          ]
        },
        {
          "severity": "nit",
          "file": "internal/cli/cmd_status.go",
          "line": 609,
          "title": "alignmentVerdictLine grows unrequested unknown-verdict handling",
          "detail": "Task 4's brief specifies alignmentLine's four cases (skipped / awaiting confirmation / confirmed-pending-verdicts / today's counts line) but says nothing about handling verdict words outside alignmentVerdictOrder. The diff adds a `seen` map plus a second pass that collects and sort.Strings' any unrecognised verdict word into a `rest` bucket (lines 616-633) — extra state and a second loop to defend against an auditor emitting a word the fixed order doesn't know. It's a reasonable robustness fix (previously such words were silently dropped) but it is scope creep beyond what the task asked for in a diff whose other status changes are otherwise minimal and tightly scoped to the brief. Flagging for awareness rather than blocking — the added complexity is small and self-contained.",
          "lenses": [
            "simplicity"
          ]
        },
        {
          "severity": "minor",
          "file": "internal/cli/cmd_status.go",
          "line": 624,
          "title": "alignmentVerdictLine's \"unknown verdict\" bucket is untested",
          "detail": "The refactor into alignmentVerdictLine adds a new branch (lines 624-633) that buckets and sorts any verdict word outside alignmentVerdictOrder rather than silently dropping it — a genuinely new code path per the doc comment (\"nothing validates the auditor's word\"). No test in internal/cli/cmd_status_test.go supplies an alignment.json verdict outside {covered, narrowed, dropped, widened, contradicted}, so this branch (and its sort.Strings ordering) has zero coverage.",
          "lenses": [
            "tests"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
